{"subjectId":"sql","id":"ctes","title":"Common Table Expressions (WITH)","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":34,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A CTE names a subquery at the top of a statement with <code>WITH name AS (…)</code>. Queries read top-down like a pipeline instead of inside-out.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH dept_stats AS (\n    SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS staff, AVG(salary) AS avg_sal\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY dept_id\n)\nSELECT d.name, s.staff, ROUND(s.avg_sal) AS avg_sal\nFROM dept_stats s\nJOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = s.dept_id\nORDER BY s.avg_sal DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name        | staff | avg_sal\n------------+-------+---------\nEngineering | 4     | 115000.0\nSales       | 3     | 84000.0\nHR          | 2     | 82500.0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Chaining CTEs</h3>\n<p>Each CTE can use the ones defined before it. A multi-step report becomes a list of named steps.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH order_totals AS (           -- step 1: value of each valid order\n    SELECT o.order_id, o.customer_id, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS total\n    FROM orders o\n    JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    WHERE o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\n    GROUP BY o.order_id, o.customer_id\n),\ncustomer_totals AS (             -- step 2: per customer\n    SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(total) AS spent\n    FROM order_totals\n    GROUP BY customer_id\n),\noverall AS (                     -- step 3: one number to compare against\n    SELECT AVG(spent) AS avg_spent FROM customer_totals\n)\nSELECT c.name, ct.orders, ct.spent,\n       CASE WHEN ct.spent &gt; o.avg_spent THEN 'above avg' ELSE 'below avg' END AS segment\nFROM customer_totals ct\nJOIN customers c ON c.customer_id = ct.customer_id\nCROSS JOIN overall o\nORDER BY ct.spent DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | orders | spent | segment\n-------+--------+-------+----------\nAnanya | 3      | 68400 | above avg\nRahul  | 2      | 61200 | above avg\nSneha  | 2      | 13700 | below avg</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>CTE vs subquery vs view vs temp table</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Scope</th><th>Reusable in the statement</th><th>Stored</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Derived table (subquery)</td><td>One place in one query</td><td>No — repeat it</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>CTE</td><td>One statement</td><td>Yes — reference by name, many times</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>View</td><td>The database</td><td>Across all queries</td><td>Definition only</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary table</td><td>Session</td><td>Across statements</td><td>Yes (data, can be indexed)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Whether a CTE is computed once (<strong>materialised</strong>) or inlined like a subquery depends on the engine. PostgreSQL 12+ inlines by default and lets you force either with <code>AS MATERIALIZED</code> / <code>AS NOT MATERIALIZED</code>; SQLite accepts the same hints.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-edit\"></i>CTEs with DML</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- give a 5% raise to everyone below their department's average\nWITH below AS (\n    SELECT e.emp_id\n    FROM employees e\n    WHERE e.salary &lt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees i WHERE i.dept_id = e.dept_id)\n)\nUPDATE employees\nSET salary = salary * 105 / 100\nWHERE emp_id IN (SELECT emp_id FROM below);\n\nSELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE emp_id IN (3, 4, 6, 7, 8) ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary\n-------+-------\nMeera  | 99750\nKaran  | 99750\nArjun  | 73500\nNeha   | 75600\nVikram | 84000</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a CTE and why use it?</summary><div><p>A Common Table Expression is a named, temporary result defined with <code>WITH</code> and visible only to the statement that follows. It improves readability (step-by-step logic), lets you reference the same intermediate result several times, and is required for recursive queries.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is a CTE faster than a subquery?</summary><div><p>Usually neither — most optimizers treat a non-recursive CTE like an inline subquery. It can be faster if it is materialised once and referenced many times, or slower if materialisation blocks predicate push-down. Choose it for clarity and check the plan when performance matters.</p></div></details></section>"}